---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MolarMass from '@/components/tools/calc2/MolarMass.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="molar-mass-calculator">
  <MolarMass client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the molar mass calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The molar mass calculator reads the formula, counts the atoms of each element (multiplying through
      brackets and hydrate parts) and adds up count × atomic weight. The result is in grams per mole, which
      is also the mass of one molecule in daltons.
    </p>
    <pre><code>molar mass = Σ (atoms of the element × its standard atomic weight)
mass percent of an element = its mass ÷ molar mass × 100</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example: glucose, C6H12O6</h2>
    <pre><code>C   6 × 12.011 =  72.066
H  12 ×  1.008 =  12.096
O   6 × 15.999 =  95.994
total          = 180.156 g/mol</code></pre>
    <p>
      Carbon is 72.066 ÷ 180.156 = 40.0% of the mass. For a hydrate, CuSO4·5H2O, the five waters add
      5 × 18.015 = 90.075 g/mol to the 159.602 g/mol of CuSO4, which gives 249.677 g/mol.
    </p>

    <h2>Atomic weights</h2>
    <p>
      The data is the IUPAC (CIAAW) table of abridged standard atomic weights for all 118 elements. Elements
      with no stable isotopes, such as technetium and the elements after uranium, use the mass number of
      their longest-lived isotope, and the result says so.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Write element symbols with the correct case: Co is cobalt, CO is carbon monoxide. Charges and states
      such as (aq) are not part of the formula. Isotope-labelled compounds have a different molar mass.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
